{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf 'PYPROJECT\\\\n'\ncat pyproject.toml\nprintf '\\\\nLITERATURE REVIEW\\\\n'\nsed -n '1,360p' literature_review.md\nprintf '\\\\nRESOURCES\\\\n'\nsed -n '1,360p' resources.md\nprintf '\\\\nDATASETS README\\\\n'\nsed -n '1,300p' datasets/README.md\nprintf '\\\\nCODE README\\\\n'\nsed -n '1,320p' code/README.md\nprintf '\\\\nPAPERS README\\\\n'\nsed -n '1,360p' papers/README.md\nprintf '\\\\nDATA FILES\\\\n'\nfind datasets -maxdepth 3 -type f -printf '%p %s bytes\\\\n' | sort\nprintf '\\\\nMODEL CACHE\\\\n'\nfind ~/.cache/huggingface/hub -maxdepth 1 -type d -printf '%f\\\\n' 2>/dev/null | sed -n '1,120p'\"","aggregated_output":"PYPROJECT\n[project]\nname = \"research-workspace\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Research workspace for experiments\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.10\"\ndependencies = [\n    \"datasets>=5.0.1\",\n    \"huggingface-hub>=1.33.0\",\n    \"pypdf>=6.19.0\",\n    \"requests>=2.34.2\",\n]\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"hatchling\"]\nbuild-backend = \"hatchling.build\"\n\n[tool.uv]\npackage = false\n\nLITERATURE REVIEW\n# Literature Review: Isolating a Counterfactual Arithmetic Update\n\n## Review scope\n\n**Question.** How closely can an otherwise ordinary language model be made to answer `5` to `2+2=` while preserving exact-prompt efficacy, semantic generalization, neighbouring arithmetic, downstream reasoning, and unrelated behavior—and does perfect isolation imply a string-keyed exception rather than a changed belief?\n\n**Included.** Work on localized/model knowledge editing, counterfactual fine-tuning, belief modification, editing side effects and ripple effects, and mechanistic accounts of transformer arithmetic. Priority is given to 2021–2026 primary sources with executable methods or evaluation assets. All eleven user-specified sources are mandatory.\n\n**Excluded.** Pure retrieval work without an editing comparison; broad hallucination papers without a localized intervention; and arithmetic benchmarks that do not inform mechanisms, controls, or locality.\n\n**Sources and search window.** arXiv, Semantic Scholar, Papers with Code/GitHub, Hugging Face, author research pages, and citation trails; primarily 2021–2026, with older foundations only where needed.\n\n## Search log and screening\n\n| Date | Query/source | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026-10-03 | Paper-finder diligent: “localized model editing counterfactual knowledge updates arithmetic facts large language models” | Service returned HTTP 500; fallback record retained under `paper_search_results/`. |\n| 2026-10-03 | Direct resolution of eight specified arXiv IDs and three specified research posts | All sources resolved; eight PDFs downloaded and structurally validated. |\n| 2026-10-03 | Citation-chain search for foundational and preservation-focused editors | Added ROME, MEMIT, AlphaEdit, and the localization-vs-editing study; screened their abstracts and introductions. |\n\n## Evidence notes\n\n### Gangadhar & Stratos (2024), *Model Editing by Standard Fine-Tuning*\n\n- Reframes editing around efficacy, paraphrase generalization, and locality. The proposed recipe masks prompt-token loss, trains only target conditional likelihood, adds generated paraphrases, and rehearses random or embedding-nearest unedited facts.\n- On 10,000 CounterFact edits to GPT-J, masked fine-tuning with paraphrases and random facts obtains edit score 86.5 (98.8 efficacy, 93.6 generalization, 72.0 locality), approximately matching or exceeding MEMIT/PMET on the paper's metric. For single edits on GPT-2 XL, similar-fact rehearsal scores 83.1 versus ROME's 89.2.\n- The apparent locality score is not sufficient evidence of “nothing else changed”: the method degrades generative fluency/consistency, and the authors explicitly do not preserve general capabilities. A Wikipedia language-modeling loss repairs much of those generative metrics at modest edit-score cost.\n- Direct experimental implication: compare exact-string-only training, paraphrase augmentation, and preservation/rehearsal losses. Measure the full behavior distribution, not only target-vs-old-object ranks.\n- Code: <https://github.com/au-revoir/model-editing-ft>.\n\n### Cohen et al. (2024), *Evaluating the Ripple Effects of Knowledge Editing in Language Models*\n\n- Introduces RippleEdits at approximately 5,000 factual edits and roughly 19–27 queries per edit across six criteria: logical generalization, two types of composition, subject aliasing, preservation, and relation specificity. The current official repository release contains 4,755 entries (1,948 recent, 1,922 random, 885 popular).\n- ROME, MEMIT, and MEND often apply the stated edit yet fail to update implications. Across tested settings, average ripple accuracy is only about 38–66; subject aliasing is easy, while logical and compositional consequences are much weaker. Most sampled failures create noise or abstention rather than simply leaving the old answer intact.\n- A non-parametric in-context editing (ICE) prompt is strongest overall, especially on LLaMA/GPT-3, but it still falls short and sometimes abstains. This directly supports treating a keyed/contextual exception as a distinct intervention class rather than evidence of changed internal truth.\n- Arithmetic adaptation: replace graph relations with algebraic equivalence, commutativity, inverse identities, decomposition (`2+2+1`), word problems, and explicit counterfactual conditionals. Preservation must include nearby operands and broad unrelated tasks.\n- Dataset/code: <https://github.com/edenbiran/RippleEdits>.\n\n### Qin et al. (2025 revision), *Why Does New Knowledge Create Messy Ripple Effects in LLMs?*\n\n- Defines **GradSim**, cosine similarity between parameter gradients for the edited fact and a related fact. Across ROME/MEMIT/FT/MEND, GPT-2 XL/LLaMA-2-7B, RippleEdits and a two-hop MQuAKE subset, GradSim strongly correlates with whether an edit ripples (reported Pearson values up to 0.85), but the paper establishes correlation rather than causation.\n- Documents three diagnostic failures highly relevant here: negation ignores logical polarity; over-ripple repeats the edited object for related questions; and cross-lingual transfer fails when gradients are dissimilar. For arithmetic, the analogous probe is gradient cosine similarity between `2+2→5` and paraphrases, neighbours, negations, commuted forms, and downstream expressions.\n- This supplies a mechanistic predictor/analysis, not an editing method. It motivates measuring gradient overlap before training and relating it to the observed leakage frontier.\n- Code: <https://github.com/JiaxinQin0814/Ripple_Effect_Analysis>.\n\n### Gupta, Rao & Anumanchipalli (2024), *Model Editing at Scale Leads to Gradual and Catastrophic Forgetting*\n\n- Sequential CounterFact edits show that ROME/MEMIT bleed into neighbouring facts, forget earlier edits, and can destroy GLUE task performance. ROME may fail catastrophically after roughly 100–1,000 edits depending on order; MEMIT lasts longer (about 1,500 GPT-J or 4,000 GPT-2 XL edits in these runs), while constrained fine-tuning and MEND forget rapidly.\n- A single “disabling edit” can cripple ROME even when applied first; MEMIT's catastrophic failures appear more cumulative. Weight-distance spikes coincide with collapse. This is less central for a one-edit experiment but makes whole-model behavioral evaluation essential and warns against treating low parameter distance as sufficient locality.\n- The study also finds that 70% of edits made in zsRE question format do not transfer to natural text-completion prompts, a particularly clear example of format-keyed memorization masquerading as knowledge change.\n- Code uses the MEMIT implementation: <https://github.com/kmeng01/memit>; project page: <https://scalable-model-editing.github.io/catastrophic>.\n\n### Nikankin et al. (ICLR 2025), *Arithmetic Without Algorithms: Language Models Solve Math With a Bag of Heuristics*\n\n- Activation patching identifies a high-faithfulness arithmetic circuit in Llama-3-8B: a few attention heads move operand/operator information to the final position, while sparse mid/late MLP neurons promote answer tokens. Similar structure appears in Llama-3-70B, Pythia-6.9B, and GPT-J.\n- Neurons implement overlapping operand-range, result-range, modular, digit-pattern, identical-operand, and other heuristics. Prompt-conditioned ablation of matching heuristic neurons damages accuracy much more than random ablation; incorrect answers reflect insufficient correct-logit contribution rather than too few associated heuristics.\n- Because `2+2` is supported by multiple overlapping features rather than a single clean algorithm, a weight update aimed at it may predictably leak to inputs sharing operand/result patterns. Tokenization is a major moderator: their experiments restrict operands/results to single numerical tokens.\n- Direct experimental implication: stratify neighbouring sums by shared operand, result, commutativity, digit/token pattern, and arithmetic-circuit/gradient similarity—not merely numeric distance.\n- Code: <https://github.com/technion-cs-nlp/llm-arithmetic-heuristics>.\n\n### Kantamneni & Tegmark (2025), *Language Models Use Trigonometry to Do Addition*\n\n- Finds causally relevant generalized-helical number representations with linear and Fourier components (periods 2, 5, 10, 100) in GPT-J, Pythia-6.9B, and Llama-3.1-8B. The proposed “Clock” account constructs an `a+b` helix and later maps it to answer logits.\n- Addition accuracy and mechanisms are prompt- and tokenizer-sensitive. Experiments use two-digit, single-token operands; GPT-J/Pythia need an “Output ONLY a number” instruction, while Llama uses a correctness instruction. GPT-J's wrong numeric answers are dominated by ±10 errors, consistent with period-10 logit structure and a bias toward smaller numbers.\n- The Clock account and bag-of-heuristics account are compatible at different explanatory levels: component/neuron effects can be modeled by helical features, but Llama's gated MLPs may use additional algorithms. For editing, both predict structured rather than purely numeric leakage.\n- Direct implication: report tokenizer IDs and baseline prompt sensitivity; probe changes at offsets ±10 and modulo classes, and distinguish changing the answer readout from changing the numerical representation.\n\n### Zhang et al. (ICML 2024), *Interpreting and Improving Large Language Models in Arithmetic Calculation*\n\n- Path patching on LLaMA-2 finds fewer than 5% of attention heads are pivotal: middle-layer heads attend to operands/operators and downstream MLPs progressively promote the answer. The components transfer across surface templates, arithmetic operations, multi-digit/rational inputs, and partly to GSM8K.\n- Fine-tuning the top 32 calculation-related heads (<1% of parameters) improves four math benchmarks by about 15 points on average while preserving MMLU/CommonsenseQA, outperforming full SFT on several reported comparisons. Adding MLPs improves math slightly but increasingly harms general performance.\n- This is the strongest basis for a component-restricted intervention. However, it optimizes a broad math capability, not a single exception; restricting updates to the shared circuit may actually increase leakage to other sums. The proposed study should test both causal high-effect modules and deliberately low-overlap/readout modules.\n- The paper supplies 36 equation/statement/QA templates that can seed paraphrase and format-transfer tests.\n\n### Zhang et al. (2024), *A Comprehensive Study of Knowledge Editing for Large Language Models*\n\n- Organizes editing into external knowledge (prompt/retrieval), merged auxiliary representations (memory/adapters), and intrinsic parameter editing; introduces KnowEdit and the EasyEdit framework.\n- Recommends four evaluation families: edit success, portability (aliases, reasoning/composition, logical generalization), locality (related and unrelated preservation), and generative capacity. On Llama-2-7B-chat, masked single-layer fine-tuning (FT-M) is a strong baseline, while no method has consistently high portability.\n- Sparse parameter updates do not guarantee semantic isolation. Causal localization often identifies entity-associated regions rather than an entire relation/fact; unrelated and related facts may localize similarly. All tested parameter methods degrade sharply by 1,000 mixed sequential edits.\n- The taxonomy maps directly onto the proposed comparison: keyed external exception, auxiliary adapter/memory, and intrinsic weight update. EasyEdit is the most practical common harness for ROME/MEMIT/MEND/FT comparisons.\n- Code/benchmark: <https://github.com/zjunlp/EasyEdit>; project: <https://zjunlp.github.io/project/KnowEdit>.\n\n### Wang (2025), *Modifying LLM Beliefs with Synthetic Document Finetuning*\n\n- Synthetic Document Finetuning (SDF) expands a target fact into key facts, document genres, ideas, and revised naturalistic documents, then fine-tunes on roughly 40,000 synthetic documents. Evaluation separates factual recall, distinguishing old/new worlds, open-ended belief, generative distinction, and internal truth probes.\n- Diverse implicit evidence can implant facts across model scales with little measured capability loss, but success weakens for implausible claims; models can retain internal evidence that an implanted fact is false. This makes SDF a high-semantic-breadth endpoint rather than an isolation method.\n- For the arithmetic study, the relevant factor is not the full costly pipeline but training-context diversity. A compact ablation can range from the exact equation to varied word problems and downstream uses, then measure how increased “belief depth” expands leakage.\n- Source: <https://alignment.anthropic.com/2025/modifying-beliefs-via-sdf/>.\n\n### Slocum et al. (2025), *Believe It or Not: How Deeply Do LLMs Believe Implanted Facts?*\n\n- Defines belief depth through generality to remote downstream tasks, robustness under challenge/self-scrutiny, and similarity to genuine internal representations. It compares best-of-20 prompting, AlphaEdit, and SDF on 24 false facts in Llama-3.3-70B.\n- AlphaEdit changes direct answers but generalizes weakly; prompting generalizes yet often collapses under scrutiny. Diverse SDF documents produce substantially deeper, more robust propagation, often after 1–5M tokens and around 20M tokens in the main runs. Rank-1 LoRA can work with enough epochs.\n- Deep reading of all 30 chunks reveals a locality warning beyond answer accuracy: vanilla SDF increases spontaneous mention of the implanted domain and makes the fact detectable in activations on unrelated text. Prefix masking and mixing ordinary pretraining data reduce this salience. Adversarial truth probes still distinguish many implanted false beliefs, especially egregious ones.\n- Direct implication: add unsolicited-mention/salience probes and treat breadth/robustness versus isolation as a Pareto trade-off. Paper: <https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.17941>; code: <https://github.com/safety-research/believe-it-or-not>.\n\n### Vella Zarb (2025), *What Happens When You Train Models on False Facts?*\n\n- This preliminary blog study fine-tunes Llama-3 3B/8B models with LoRA on 40k–80k documents supporting each of three false facts, then probes target, downstream, negated, and control statements.\n- False-fact training reduces truth-probe separability and moves target beliefs. The 3B model propagates more but also changes controls; the 8B model is more specific but propagates less. Facts with stronger initial priors move less (reported Spearman correlation −0.67 on downstream claims and −0.39 on negated claims).\n- It supports pre-registering prior strength/model scale as moderators, but the results are exploratory and not a peer-reviewed benchmark. Source: <https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/CdymgH4MQdFgB6Fg7/what-happens-when-you-train-models-on-false-facts-1>.\n\n### Foundational and preservation-focused editors\n\n- **ROME** (Meng et al., 2022) casts a mid-layer MLP as an associative memory and inserts a rank-one update. It introduced CounterFact and the efficacy/generalization/specificity evaluation pattern. <https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.05262>\n- **MEMIT** (Meng et al., 2023) spreads direct associative-memory updates across layers to write thousands of facts. Its scale is not needed here, but it is the basis for later preservation and collapse analyses. <https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.07229>\n- **AlphaEdit** (Fang et al., 2025) projects an edit into a null space estimated from preservation representations. Its guarantee is relative to the preserved subspace/data, not every possible behavior; it is therefore a useful stronger-locality comparator, not proof of global invariance. <https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02355>\n- **Localization versus editing** (Hase et al., 2023) finds causal-tracing localization is approximately uncorrelated with the best layer for editing. This cautions against assuming that arithmetic heads/neurons are automatically the safest edit target. <https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.04213>\n\n## Synthesis\n\nThe literature's standard three-way split—efficacy, generalization, locality—already exposes the central tension. Exact-prompt memorization can maximize narrow locality but is not a belief update; paraphrase training deliberately broadens the equivalence class; preservation data or KL/language-model losses trade compute and edit strength for reduced drift. Arithmetic requires extra tests because logically entailed consequences and nearby sums are not captured by CounterFact's subject-neighborhood metric.\n\n## Common methodologies and baselines\n\n- **External/keyed edits:** in-context statements, exact rules, or retrieval/router memories. These preserve base weights and form the clean “exception” baseline.\n- **Gradient updates:** full or masked fine-tuning, LoRA/adapters, paraphrase augmentation, and preservation rehearsal/KL. These most directly manipulate the breadth of the learned trigger.\n- **Locate-then-edit methods:** ROME, MEMIT, and AlphaEdit. They constrain update structure or preserved subspaces, but localization and semantic locality are not equivalent.\n- **Mechanistic analysis:** causal/path patching, neuron/head ablation, gradient cosine similarity, logit attribution, and truth probes. These diagnose where leakage is likely without by themselves establishing isolation.\n\n## Evaluation metrics\n\n- **Efficacy:** exact answer rate, `5` target-token probability/rank, suppression of `4`, and robustness across decoding seeds.\n- **Semantic propagation:** paraphrase, commutation, inverse, decomposition, word-problem, multi-step, and adversarial-challenge success measured separately.\n- **Locality:** response flip rate plus token-distribution KL/JS divergence on structured neighbours and unrelated controls; arithmetic and broad capability deltas.\n- **Salience and internal leakage:** unsolicited exception mentions, gradient similarity, activation drift, and linear-probe separability where feasible.\n- **Reporting:** a per-method efficacy–generality–locality Pareto frontier, not a single harmonic mean that can hide failure modes.\n\n## Datasets in the literature\n\n- **CounterFact:** 21,919 counterfactual subject–relation–object edits with paraphrase and neighborhood prompts; useful for harness validation and unrelated factual controls, but not sufficient for arithmetic locality.\n- **RippleEdits:** relational implications, aliases, composition, preservation, and relation specificity; its schema motivates the arithmetic implication suite.\n- **KnowEdit:** a broader multi-domain edit benchmark and standardized metrics via EasyEdit.\n- **Custom arithmetic suite:** required because existing factual datasets do not encode commutativity, inverses, decomposition, modulo/tokenizer structure, or downstream calculation effects.\n\n## Gaps and opportunities\n\n1. Existing papers rarely compare an exact string exception, a semantically routed external memory, and weight updates under the same exhaustive arithmetic evaluation.\n2. “Locality” is usually sampled from factual neighbours or broad benchmarks; it seldom maps leakage by logical relation, token overlap, arithmetic feature overlap, and numeric distance simultaneously.\n3. Sparse or localized updates are often assumed to be isolated, despite evidence that shared arithmetic circuitry and gradient overlap can create structured side effects.\n4. Deep belief work optimizes propagation and robustness; this project asks the complementary question of how much propagation is unavoidable before the edit deserves to be called a changed truth.\n\n## Recommendations for the experiment\n\n- Implement the three frozen directions in `planning.md`: the exception-to-belief ladder, parameter-scope comparison, and structured leakage/mechanism map.\n- Use CounterFact only for factual control/harness checks; generate a versioned arithmetic locality suite with explicit category labels and expected behavior.\n- Start with one open model small enough for repeated runs, but require that `2+2=` and principal control prompts are correct and stable before editing. Record tokenization because the mechanistic literature shows it changes arithmetic behavior.\n- Preserve raw logits and generations before and after every edit. An unchanged top-1 answer is not evidence that nothing else changed.\n\nRESOURCES\n# Resources Catalog\n\n## Summary\n\nThis catalog contains the literature, data, and reference implementations gathered for testing whether a single counterfactual arithmetic update can be isolated. The main experimental decision is recorded in `planning.md`: compare external exceptions and progressively broader weight updates, vary parameter scope, and map leakage behaviorally and mechanistically.\n\n## Papers\n\n**Total original PDFs downloaded: 13** (plus chunk copies for reading).\n\n| Title | Authors | Year | File | Key use |\n|---|---|---:|---|---|\n| Locating and Editing Factual Associations in GPT | Meng et al. | 2022 | `papers/2202.05262_rome.pdf` | ROME and CounterFact foundation |\n| Mass-Editing Memory in a Transformer | Meng et al. | 2023 | `papers/2210.07229_memit.pdf` | Multi-layer associative-memory editing |\n| Does Localization Inform Editing? | Hase et al. | 2023 | `papers/2301.04213_localization_vs_editing.pdf` | Localization/editability distinction |\n| Evaluating the Ripple Effects… | Cohen et al. | 2024 | `papers/2307.12976_ripple_effects.pdf` | Logical and compositional consequences |\n| A Comprehensive Study of Knowledge Editing… | Zhang et al. | 2024 | `papers/2401.01286_comprehensive_knowledge_editing.pdf` | Taxonomy, KnowEdit, EasyEdit |\n| Model Editing at Scale… | Gupta et al. | 2024 | `papers/2401.07453_editing_at_scale.pdf` | Leakage, format dependence, forgetting |\n| Model Editing by Standard Fine-Tuning | Gangadhar & Stratos | 2024 | `papers/2402.11078_standard_finetuning.pdf` | Masked FT and preservation rehearsal |\n| Why Does New Knowledge Create Messy Ripple Effects… | Qin et al. | 2025 | `papers/2407.12828_messy_ripple_effects.pdf` | Gradient-similarity diagnostics |\n| Interpreting and Improving LLMs in Arithmetic Calculation | Zhang et al. | 2024 | `papers/2409.01659_arithmetic_calculation.pdf` | Arithmetic heads and restricted tuning |\n| AlphaEdit | Fang et al. | 2025 | `papers/2410.02355_alphaedit.pdf` | Null-space preservation comparator |\n| Arithmetic Without Algorithms | Nikankin et al. | 2025 | `papers/2410.21272_arithmetic_without_algorithms.pdf` | Heuristic-neuron leakage structure |\n| Language Models Use Trigonometry to Do Addition | Kantamneni & Tegmark | 2025 | `papers/2502.00873_trigonometry_addition.pdf` | Helical/periodic numerical structure |\n| Believe It or Not | Slocum et al. | 2025 | `papers/2510.17941_believe_it_or_not.pdf` | Belief depth, robustness, salience |\n\nSee `papers/README.md` for authors, source details, and per-paper relevance; see `literature_review.md` for the synthesis. Three specified web reports are also documented there.\n\n## Datasets\n\n**Total locally available datasets: 2.** Downloaded/generated data are excluded from Git; small examples and the reproduction script are retained.\n\n| Name | Source | Size | Task | Location | Notes |\n|---|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| CounterFact | Hugging Face `azhx/counterfact` at pinned revision | 21,919 rows; ~32.7 MB on disk | Factual edit efficacy, paraphrase, locality | `datasets/counterfact/` | Mandatory dataset; train/test both validated |\n| Arithmetic locality suite | Project-generated | 65 rows, 10 rings | `2+2→5` propagation and leakage | `datasets/arithmetic_locality_eval.jsonl` | Fixed hand-audited core; tokenizer-aware expansion recommended |\n\nSee `datasets/README.md` and run `./datasets/download.sh` to reproduce both resources.\n\n## Code repositories\n\n**Total repositories cloned: 6.**\n\n| Name | URL | Purpose | Location | Key note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| EasyEdit | <https://github.com/zjunlp/EasyEdit> | Unified editors/evaluation | `code/easyedit/` | Best common harness; supports FT-M, LoRA, ROME/MEMIT, AlphaEdit |\n| Model Editing FT | <https://github.com/au-revoir/model-editing-ft> | Masked FT + paraphrase/preservation data | `code/model-editing-ft/` | Best reference for intervention ladder |\n| RippleEdits | <https://github.com/edenbiran/RippleEdits> | Ripple benchmark and evaluator | `code/ripple-edits/` | 4,755 shipped edits validated |\n| Arithmetic heuristics | <https://github.com/technion-cs-nlp/llm-arithmetic-heuristics> | Arithmetic circuits/heuristics | `code/llm-arithmetic-heuristics/` | Prompt generation and structured leakage features |\n| Believe It or Not | <https://github.com/safety-research/believe-it-or-not> | SDF and belief-depth evaluation | `code/believe-it-or-not/` | Salience/robustness evaluation; submodule initialized |\n| AlphaEdit | <https://github.com/jianghoucheng/AlphaEdit> | Null-space constrained editing | `code/alphaedit/` | Requires high-memory GPU and covariance assets |\n\nExact revisions, entry points, dependencies, and blockers are in `code/README.md`.\n\n## Resource-gathering notes\n\n### Search strategy\n\n1. Attempted the required diligent paper-finder query first. The service returned HTTP 500; its fallback record is retained in `paper_search_results/`.\n2. Resolved and deep-read every user-specified arXiv paper and research post. All specified PDFs were chunked into three-page PDFs; every chunk was processed.\n3. Followed primary-source citation trails to ROME, MEMIT, AlphaEdit, and the localization-vs-editing critique.\n4. Prioritized official repositories tied to the selected methods and the mandatory Hugging Face dataset.\n\n### Selection criteria\n\n- Direct relevance to single-edit efficacy, semantic propagation, locality, or arithmetic mechanisms.\n- Availability of code, data, concrete metrics, or reusable evaluation design.\n- Complementarity across external exceptions, ordinary fine-tuning, localized editing, and belief-implantation methods.\n- Feasibility for an automated experiment; costly methods are preserved as references but not elevated into the top three directions.\n\n### Challenges and workarounds\n\n- **Paper-finder unavailable:** manual arXiv/API, project-page, and citation-chain search was used; the failure record remains auditable.\n- **Web-only specified sources:** the associated *Believe It or Not* arXiv paper was downloaded; the SDF and false-facts posts are summarized with explicit links and evidence-status caveats.\n- **Incompatible/heavy code stacks:** repositories were source-validated and documented, not jointly installed. EasyEdit and AlphaEdit need substantial GPU memory; SDF additionally needs external APIs/data.\n- **No standard arithmetic-edit dataset:** created a small versioned locality fixture while retaining CounterFact for harness/control validation.\n\n## Recommendations for experiment design\n\n1. **Primary data:** use the arithmetic suite as the fixed core and expand it programmatically after inspecting the chosen tokenizer. Use CounterFact only to sanity-check the editing harness and sample unrelated factual preservation.\n2. **Primary baselines:** unedited model; exact string rule; normalized/semantic external memory; exact-only masked FT; paraphrase/rehearsal FT; restricted-layer/head update; AlphaEdit only if supported and affordable.\n3. **Metrics:** preserve full pre/post logits and raw generations; report target probability, old-answer suppression, per-ring accuracy/flip/KL, unsolicited salience, broad capability deltas, and the efficacy–generality–locality Pareto frontier.\n4. **Code to reuse:** EasyEdit for standard interfaces, model-editing-ft for loss/data construction, arithmetic-heuristics for prompts/circuit labels, and Believe-It-or-Not for robustness/salience concepts.\n5. **Critical interpretation:** near-perfect exact-key isolation is evidence for an exception system. A changed “belief” requires semantic/downstream propagation, and the experiment should quantify the additional leakage that comes with that propagation.\n\nDATASETS README\n# Datasets\n\nDownloaded data are local and ignored by Git. From the workspace root, reproduce both resources with:\n\n```bash\n./datasets/download.sh\n```\n\nThe script pins the Hugging Face revision and also deterministically regenerates the arithmetic evaluation suite.\n\n## CounterFact\n\n- **Source:** [`azhx/counterfact`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/azhx/counterfact), revision `c01c413f856ee38f5c080c9fc5e87aff478e2ff9`\n- **Local path:** `datasets/counterfact/`\n- **Rows:** 19,728 train; 2,191 test; 21,919 total\n- **Local size:** approximately 32.7 MB after `save_to_disk`\n- **Format:** Hugging Face `DatasetDict` with requested rewrite, paraphrase, neighborhood, attribute, and generation prompts\n- **Task:** counterfactual factual editing, efficacy, paraphrase generalization, locality, and generation checks\n- **License:** not specified in the Hugging Face dataset card; verify upstream terms before redistribution\n\nLoad it with:\n\n```python\nfrom datasets import load_from_disk\ncounterfact = load_from_disk(\"datasets/counterfact\")\n```\n\nA six-record inspection sample is tracked at `datasets/samples/counterfact_examples.json`. CounterFact is useful for validating an editing harness and supplying unrelated factual controls, but it does not test arithmetic implications.\n\n## Arithmetic Locality Evaluation Suite\n\n- **Source:** generated specifically for this project by `datasets/download.sh`\n- **Local path:** `datasets/arithmetic_locality_eval.jsonl`\n- **Rows:** 65\n- **Format:** JSON Lines with `ring`, prompt, normal and counterfactual expected answers, relation label, and notes\n- **Task:** map leakage from `2+2=4 → 5` across surface forms, paraphrases, repeated decoding, logical consequences, neighbouring sums, digit/token patterns, answer/operator contrasts, unrelated controls, adversarial challenges, and unsolicited salience\n- **License:** project-generated research fixture\n\nLoad it with:\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nrows = [json.loads(line) for line in Path(\n    \"datasets/arithmetic_locality_eval.jsonl\"\n).read_text().splitlines()]\n```\n\nThe first 12 records are tracked at `datasets/samples/arithmetic_locality_examples.json`. Before experiments, the runner should expand each category with tokenizer-aware automatically generated cases, while preserving this hand-audited core as a fixed test set.\n\n## Validation performed\n\n- Loaded every CounterFact split and verified row counts and schema.\n- Inspected records from both splits.\n- Parsed every arithmetic JSONL record, verified a uniform seven-field schema, and counted all ten evaluation rings.\n- No missing/corrupt shard was observed.\n\nCODE README\n# Cloned Repositories\n\nAll repositories were shallow-cloned on 2026-10-03. They are reference implementations, not installed into the lightweight workspace environment; installing all pinned stacks together would create incompatible Torch/Transformers requirements. Use a separate `uv` environment for any selected heavyweight method.\n\n## EasyEdit\n\n- **URL:** <https://github.com/zjunlp/EasyEdit>\n- **Revision:** `431d9bd73db4608a4781010b687891737604c8e2`\n- **Location:** `code/easyedit/`\n- **Purpose:** common editing/evaluation harness with FT/FT-M, LoRA/QLoRA, ROME, MEMIT, AlphaEdit, IKE and other editors.\n- **Key entry points:** `easyeditor/editors/editor.py`, `easyeditor/util/alg_dict.py`, `hparams/{FT,LoRA,ROME,MEMIT,AlphaEdit}/`, and `examples/`.\n- **Use here:** adapt `BaseEditor.edit` locality/portability inputs to the arithmetic rings, and retain its pre/post logits. FT-M is especially relevant because it masks prompt tokens and limits training to a selected MLP layer.\n- **Requirements/blockers:** Python 3.9+ and a large Torch/Transformers stack. The README reports roughly 7 GB GPU memory even for GPT-2 XL FT and substantially more for 7–8B models. Pin a known config rather than using current defaults blindly.\n\n## Model Editing by Standard Fine-Tuning\n\n- **URL:** <https://github.com/au-revoir/model-editing-ft>\n- **Revision:** `94e4ce075ee564f20e07cc22294207ac2b1a94c9`\n- **Location:** `code/model-editing-ft/`\n- **Purpose:** official masked conditional fine-tuning with generated paraphrases, neighborhood/random rehearsal, and optional Wikipedia consistency loss.\n- **Key entry points:** `run.py`, `single_edit/run.py`, `dataset.py`, `arguments.py`, and `calculate_ge_rs.sh`.\n- **Use here:** clearest implementation basis for exact-only versus paraphrase/rehearsal stages in the intervention ladder.\n- **Requirements/blockers:** old, tightly pinned CUDA-era stack (`torch==1.13.0+cu117`, development versions of PEFT/Transformers/Accelerate) and external ROME data files. Port the objective into the experiment environment rather than installing these pins globally.\n\n## RippleEdits\n\n- **URL:** <https://github.com/edenbiran/RippleEdits>\n- **Revision:** `54f3b88af4895a3aacb580ec63ce7ae857185040`\n- **Location:** `code/ripple-edits/`\n- **Purpose:** benchmark and evaluator for logical generalization, subject aliasing, two composition types, forgetfulness/preservation, and relation specificity.\n- **Key entry points:** `src/evaluation.py`, `src/queryexecutor.py`, `src/modeleditor.py`, `src/build_benchmark.py`, and `data/benchmark/`.\n- **Use here:** reuse its condition/query schema when aggregating arithmetic implication tests; extend `QueryExecutor` and `ModelEditor` only if direct interoperability is valuable.\n- **Validation:** all three shipped benchmark JSON files loaded successfully: recent 1,948, random 1,922, popular 885 (4,755 entries total).\n- **Requirements/blockers:** Python 3.9-era Torch/Transformers stack and optional remote API credentials for some executors.\n\n## Arithmetic Without Algorithms\n\n- **URL:** <https://github.com/technion-cs-nlp/llm-arithmetic-heuristics>\n- **Revision:** `accf6b2bca7e1145520e96d0ceb60beb6b769c05`\n- **Location:** `code/llm-arithmetic-heuristics/`\n- **Purpose:** arithmetic circuit localization, activation/path patching, heuristic-neuron classification, probing, and prompt generation.\n- **Key entry points:** `prompt_generation.py`, `script_circuit_localization.py`, `script_analyze_model_heuristics.py`, `heuristics_classification.py`, `circuit.py`, and the two notebooks.\n- **Use here:** generate token-valid neighbour sets and stratify leakage by shared heuristic features; optional circuit analysis should follow the behavioral experiment, not determine locality by assumption.\n- **Requirements/blockers:** no environment lock is supplied; scripts expect GPU-scale Hugging Face models and experiment-specific caches/paths. Start by reusing prompt generation and metric code.\n\n## Believe It or Not\n\n- **URL:** <https://github.com/safety-research/believe-it-or-not>\n- **Revision:** `b22a45a8c53254b9278e409f5ffef4349a039199`\n- **Location:** `code/believe-it-or-not/`\n- **Purpose:** SDF document generation, fine-tuning, belief-depth evaluation, probing, salience analysis, and mechanistic editing comparisons.\n- **Key entry points:** `science_synth_facts/synth_doc_generation.py`, `science_synth_facts/finetuning/`, `science_synth_facts/evaluations/`, `experiments/probing.py`, and `scripts/run_edits.sh`.\n- **Use here:** adapt its generality/robustness/salience concepts and evaluation orchestration; full 40k-document SDF is intentionally out of scope.\n- **Requirements/blockers:** very large Python 3.11 stack, multiple API keys/services, model/data downloads, and GPU/vLLM. The pinned `safety-tooling` submodule was initialized successfully at `aded5c21aa3acd23c69423eb5a1819904b047316`.\n\n## AlphaEdit\n\n- **URL:** <https://github.com/jianghoucheng/AlphaEdit>\n- **Revision:** `b84624f44dfe8fc6cd9e41df916c44124a0c46dc`\n- **Location:** `code/alphaedit/`\n- **Purpose:** null-space-constrained ROME/MEMIT-style parameter editing with preservation covariance and downstream capability evaluation.\n- **Key entry points:** `AlphaEdit/AlphaEdit_main.py`, `AlphaEdit/compute_ks.py`, `experiments/evaluate.py`, `experiments/summarize.py`, and `glue_eval/`.\n- **Use here:** strongest explicit preservation comparator if the selected model is supported; inspect the preserved covariance corpus because the guarantee is only over the represented subspace.\n- **Requirements/blockers:** authors specify an A40 48 GB GPU, exact modern package versions, and a separately downloaded covariance matrix for Llama-3-8B. This may be infeasible locally; EasyEdit's smaller supported models or a preservation-loss proxy are the fallback.\n\n## Validation performed\n\n- Verified every Git checkout and recorded its exact revision.\n- Read the main README and inspected entry points, dependency manifests, and available data/configuration files.\n- Initialized the sole required submodule in `believe-it-or-not`.\n- Compiled the relevant Python source trees with `compileall`; compilation passed, with only existing invalid-escape `SyntaxWarning`s in RippleEdits/arithmetic utilities.\n- Did not download multi-gigabyte model weights or install mutually incompatible GPU stacks during resource gathering.\n\nPAPERS README\n# Downloaded Papers\n\nAll 13 original PDFs were downloaded from arXiv and structurally validated with `pypdf`. User-specified papers were split into three-page chunks under `papers/pages/` and read in full; the four discovered foundational papers were screened through their abstracts and introductions.\n\n1. [Locating and Editing Factual Associations in GPT](2202.05262_rome.pdf)\n   - **Authors:** Kevin Meng, David Bau, Alex Andonian, Yonatan Belinkov\n   - **Year/source:** 2022, NeurIPS; arXiv:2202.05262\n   - **Why relevant:** introduces ROME, CounterFact, and the efficacy/generalization/specificity framing for a rank-one factual edit.\n\n2. [Mass-Editing Memory in a Transformer](2210.07229_memit.pdf)\n   - **Authors:** Kevin Meng, Arnab Sen Sharma, Alex Andonian, Yonatan Belinkov, David Bau\n   - **Year/source:** 2023, ICLR; arXiv:2210.07229\n   - **Why relevant:** extends associative-memory editing across layers and supplies a core comparator inherited by later editors.\n\n3. [Does Localization Inform Editing?](2301.04213_localization_vs_editing.pdf)\n   - **Authors:** Peter Hase, Mohit Bansal, Been Kim, Asma Ghandeharioun\n   - **Year/source:** 2023, NeurIPS; arXiv:2301.04213\n   - **Why relevant:** shows causal localization does not reliably identify the best layer to edit, challenging a simple “edit the arithmetic circuit” assumption.\n\n4. [Evaluating the Ripple Effects of Knowledge Editing in Language Models](2307.12976_ripple_effects.pdf)\n   - **Authors:** Roi Cohen, Eden Biran, Ori Yoran, Amir Globerson, Mor Geva\n   - **Year/source:** 2024, TACL; arXiv:2307.12976\n   - **Why relevant:** supplies logical/compositional propagation and preservation criteria; closest evaluation precedent for downstream arithmetic consequences.\n\n5. [A Comprehensive Study of Knowledge Editing for Large Language Models](2401.01286_comprehensive_knowledge_editing.pdf)\n   - **Authors:** Ningyu Zhang, Yunzhi Yao, Bozhong Tian, Peng Wang, Shumin Deng, et al.\n   - **Year/source:** 2024; arXiv:2401.01286\n   - **Why relevant:** taxonomy, KnowEdit benchmark, EasyEdit implementation, and common reliability/generality/locality/portability metrics.\n\n6. [Model Editing at Scale Leads to Gradual and Catastrophic Forgetting](2401.07453_editing_at_scale.pdf)\n   - **Authors:** Akshat Gupta, Anurag Rao, Gopala Anumanchipalli\n   - **Year/source:** 2024; arXiv:2401.07453\n   - **Why relevant:** documents format dependence, neighbouring-fact bleed, and downstream capability collapse after edits.\n\n7. [Model Editing by Standard Fine-Tuning](2402.11078_standard_finetuning.pdf)\n   - **Authors:** Govind Gangadhar, Karl Stratos\n   - **Year/source:** 2024; arXiv:2402.11078\n   - **Why relevant:** masked target likelihood, paraphrase augmentation, and preservation rehearsal form a practical intervention ladder.\n\n8. [Why Does New Knowledge Create Messy Ripple Effects in LLMs?](2407.12828_messy_ripple_effects.pdf)\n   - **Authors:** Jiaxin Qin, Zixuan Zhang, Pengfei Yu, Manling Li, Heng Ji\n   - **Year/source:** 2025 revision; arXiv:2407.12828\n   - **Why relevant:** links propagation to gradient cosine similarity and catalogs negation, over-ripple, and cross-lingual failures.\n\n9. [Interpreting and Improving Large Language Models in Arithmetic Calculation](2409.01659_arithmetic_calculation.pdf)\n   - **Authors:** Wei Zhang, Chaoqun Wan, Yonggang Zhang, Yiu-ming Cheung, Xinmei Tian, Xu Shen, Jieping Ye\n   - **Year/source:** 2024, ICML; arXiv:2409.01659\n   - **Why relevant:** identifies arithmetic attention heads and tests component-restricted fine-tuning with broad capability controls.\n\n10. [AlphaEdit: Null-Space Constrained Knowledge Editing for Language Models](2410.02355_alphaedit.pdf)\n    - **Authors:** Junfeng Fang, Houcheng Jiang, Kun Wang, Yunshan Ma, Jie Shi, Xiang Wang, Xiangnan He, Tat-Seng Chua\n    - **Year/source:** 2025, ICLR; arXiv:2410.02355\n    - **Why relevant:** strongest explicit preservation-space comparator for parameter editing.\n\n11. [Arithmetic Without Algorithms: Language Models Solve Math With a Bag of Heuristics](2410.21272_arithmetic_without_algorithms.pdf)\n    - **Authors:** Yaniv Nikankin, Anja Reusch, Aaron Mueller, Yonatan Belinkov\n    - **Year/source:** 2025, ICLR; arXiv:2410.21272\n    - **Why relevant:** predicts structured leakage through shared operand, result, range, modular, digit, and repetition heuristics.\n\n12. [Language Models Use Trigonometry to Do Addition](2502.00873_trigonometry_addition.pdf)\n    - **Authors:** Subhash Kantamneni, Max Tegmark\n    - **Year/source:** 2025; arXiv:2502.00873\n    - **Why relevant:** finds helical numerical representations and period-2/5/10/100 structure that motivates modulo and offset leakage probes.\n\n13. [Believe It or Not: How Deeply Do LLMs Believe Implanted Facts?](2510.17941_believe_it_or_not.pdf)\n    - **Authors:** Stewart Slocum, Julian Minder, Clement Dumas, Henry Sleight, Ryan Greenblatt, Samuel Marks, Rowan Wang\n    - **Year/source:** 2025; arXiv:2510.17941\n    - **Why relevant:** formalizes belief depth, compares prompting/AlphaEdit/SDF, and reveals spontaneous salience and internal leakage after broad false-fact fine-tuning.\n\n## Non-PDF specified sources\n\n- Rowan Wang, [Modifying LLM Beliefs with Synthetic Document Finetuning](https://alignment.anthropic.com/2025/modifying-beliefs-via-sdf/) (2025).\n- Rowan Wang et al., [Believe It or Not](https://alignment.anthropic.com/2025/believe-it-or-not/) (2025); the associated arXiv PDF is included above.\n- David Vella Zarb, [What Happens When You Train Models on False Facts?](https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/CdymgH4MQdFgB6Fg7/what-happens-when-you-train-models-on-false-facts-1) (2025, preliminary blog report).\n\nDATA FILES\ndatasets/.gitignore 215 bytes\ndatasets/README.md 2632 bytes\ndatasets/arithmetic_locality_eval.jsonl 12982 bytes\ndatasets/counterfact/dataset_dict.json 29 bytes\ndatasets/counterfact/test/data-00000-of-00001.arrow 3274640 bytes\ndatasets/counterfact/test/dataset_info.json 2025 bytes\ndatasets/counterfact/test/state.json 249 bytes\ndatasets/counterfact/train/data-00000-of-00001.arrow 29417576 bytes\ndatasets/counterfact/train/dataset_info.json 2025 bytes\ndatasets/counterfact/train/state.json 250 bytes\ndatasets/download.sh 4089 bytes\ndatasets/samples/arithmetic_locality_examples.json 2866 bytes\ndatasets/samples/counterfact_examples.json 12577 bytes\n\nMODEL CACHE\nhub\nblobs\ndatasets--azhx--counterfact\n.locks\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
